WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.286
The Purdue Enterprise Reference Architecture is an industrial architecture model that organizes

00:00:04.286 --> 00:00:06.727
systems according to the functions they perform,

00:00:06.727 --> 00:00:10.564
from the physical process through control and operations to enterprise planning.

00:00:10.564 --> 00:00:11.610
In security discussions,

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:16.494
the term Purdue Model usually refers to this layered view of an industrial environment.

00:00:16.494 --> 00:00:20.679
The model gives engineers, operators, architects, and security teams a shared vocabulary.

00:00:20.679 --> 00:00:24.516
Instead of describing a network only by subnets or vendor products,

00:00:24.516 --> 00:00:28.353
teams can discuss where a function belongs, what it depends on,

00:00:28.353 --> 00:00:31.143
and how close it is to physical control.

00:00:31.143 --> 00:00:35.329
A controller at Level 1 has a different operational purpose and consequence

00:00:35.329 --> 00:00:37.770
than an enterprise application at Level 4.

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:42.305
OT security practitioners use the model to reason about trust boundaries, communication paths,

00:00:42.305 --> 00:00:44.049
access, monitoring, and incident containment.

00:00:44.049 --> 00:00:45.793
It helps expose questionable architecture.

00:00:45.793 --> 00:00:46.490
For example,

00:00:46.490 --> 00:00:50.676
a user workstation should not normally communicate directly with a field controller

00:00:50.676 --> 00:00:52.769
simply because both devices use Ethernet.

00:00:52.769 --> 00:00:57.652
The Purdue Model is not a product, a protocol, or a complete security design.

00:00:57.652 --> 00:01:00.094
It does not prescribe every firewall rule,

00:01:00.094 --> 00:01:04.628
and assigning a system to a level does not make that system secure.

00:01:04.628 --> 00:01:08.814
The model must be combined with asset inventory, current diagrams, operational requirements,

00:01:08.814 --> 00:01:10.558
risk analysis, and engineering judgment.

00:01:10.558 --> 00:01:14.046
Modern environments also include cloud analytics, wireless devices, vendor platforms,

00:01:14.046 --> 00:01:14.743
remote operations,

00:01:14.743 --> 00:01:19.975
and industrial Internet of Things systems that may not fit neatly into a simple stack.

00:01:19.975 --> 00:01:23.114
Use Purdue as a reference for asking better questions,

00:01:23.114 --> 00:01:27.300
not as a perfect map that forces every architecture into identical boxes.
